35,2,1,talk,Asuka,,"My research shows that a large annual commitment deters potential new members. Furthermore, previous attempts at tiered membership caused friction and discouraged those in lower grades from participating. We should be cautious about rigid structures.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Benjamin,,"My findings suggest that the administrative burden is heavily skewed. Chasing payments for individual sessions is incredibly time-consuming compared to a yearly collection, though some members do struggle with a single lump sum.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"The data confirms a significant imbalance. A small minority of members account for the vast majority of attendance and handle all the operational work, while many others rarely, if ever, show up.","{""words"":32,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,Shunichi,,It's clear that charging per event is a mistake. Turnout dropped significantly after the summer outings because people stopped feeling like they belonged. We're creating a barrier to entry that kills the club's spirit.,"{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Takumi,,"The money is mostly gone before anyone even walks through the door; seventy percent is fixed costs. Plus, calculating part-year fees for mid-year joiners is a total mess. We need a system that covers the basics without the headache.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Benjamin, if chasing individual payments is that draining, we can't go with activity-based charging. However, @Takumi, since fixed costs are so high, we must ensure the base fee is stable. We need a balance.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Benjamin"",""Takumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Benjamin,,"Indeed, @Asuka. The administrative cost of activity-based charging is simply too high for any sane treasurer to bear. However, we must also consider those who find a single annual payment prohibitive.","{""words"":31,""mentions"":[""Asuka""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"If we have a small group doing all the work and attending everything, while others pay the same but never appear, a flat fee feels unfair to those carrying the club. We must address this disparity.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, fairness is important, but not at the cost of the club's soul. If we make people weigh the cost of every visit, they'll just stop coming. We need to make belonging feel automatic again.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, I get that it feels unfair, but we can't ignore that seventy percent of our costs are fixed. If we cut the fee for people who don't show up, the regulars will just end up paying more.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Asuka,,"@Jonathan, if we move away from a flat fee, we risk the friction I found in the minute books. Tiered levels often create a social divide where lower-tier members feel like second-class citizens and stop contributing.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Benjamin,,"@Jonathan, while I sympathize with the desire for fairness, we must weigh it against the treasurer's sanity. A complex system to reward high attendance might just create more administrative chaos. Logic suggests simplicity is our best ally.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"I understand the need for simplicity, but we cannot ignore that a few people are doing all the work. If we don't acknowledge this imbalance, we risk burning out the very people who keep the club alive.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,Shunichi,,"@Jonathan, burnout is real, but the solution isn't a fee structure that makes people hesitate. If we make it a transaction every time they walk in, we lose the community. We need a system that encourages presence.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Takumi,,"@Jonathan, if we make it too complex to ""reward"" the regulars, we're just adding more work for the treasurer. We need a system that's easy to manage and actually covers those fixed costs.","{""words"":33,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Asuka,,"We are facing a conflict between administrative ease, financial stability, and social cohesion. Given the risks of tiered friction and the failure of per-event charges, a simplified approach seems most logical to preserve the club.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Benjamin,,"I agree. When we weigh the treasurer's burden and the risk of alienating members, a flat fee is the most rational choice, provided we find a way to accommodate those who cannot pay in one lump sum.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I still worry about the resentment of the regulars, but I cannot argue with the administrative reality. If the alternatives cause more friction or burnout for the treasurer, a flat fee is the most stable path.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,Shunichi,,"Exactly. If we want people to stop hesitating and start feeling like they belong again, we need to remove the mental barrier of a price tag at the door. A flat fee is the only way to restore that.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Takumi,,I'm glad we're finally on the same page. Let's stop overcomplicating it. A flat fee covers our fixed costs and stops the treasurer from losing their mind. It's the only way to be sure we have the money.,"{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
